# Project workflow

## Goal

Build the epilot BTC/USD prediction assignment incrementally.
Deliver a reliable, understandable and deployed MVP before optional modes.

## Required context

Before starting work:
- Read docs/implementation-plan.md.
- Identify the active ticket and its acceptance criteria.
- Read relevant sections of docs/architecture.md.
- Inspect existing code before proposing changes.

Do not assume ordinary documentation is automatically loaded.
Explicitly read the relevant files.

## Ticket execution

- Work on one ticket at a time.
- Before editing, explain the approach and affected files briefly.
- Implement only the active ticket.
- Preserve unrelated existing changes.
- Prefer simple code and small, reviewable changes.
- Run appropriate checks and fix failures introduced by your changes.
- Never claim a check passed unless it actually ran.
- Do not begin the next ticket automatically.
- Do not commit or push unless user explicitly asks.

After implementing a ticket, report:
1. What changed and why.
2. Checks run and their results.
3. Exact manual testing steps.
4. Remaining limitations or decisions.
5. Suggested commit message.

Mark the ticket Awaiting manual verification.
Stop and wait for user to test.
Mark it Complete only after user confirms.

## Documentation maintenance

After each ticket:
- Update its status in docs/implementation-plan.md.
- Add a short factual implementation diary entry.

When relevant:
- Update docs/architecture.md for changes to decisions, API contracts,
  data models, workflows, scoring, security or recovery.
- Update README.md for changes to functionality, setup, commands,
  testing or deployment.

Describe actual implementation honestly.
Clearly distinguish planned behaviour from implemented behaviour.
Do not create additional documentation files without a clear reason.

## Core game requirements

- New players start with score 0.
- Show backend score and latest available BTC/USD price.
- Allow Up or Down.
- Permit only one active prediction per player.
- Enforce the active-prediction restriction in the backend.
- The backend owns entry prices, timestamps and deadlines.
- Do not trust client-supplied scores, prices or results.
- Do not settle before at least 60 seconds have elapsed.
- Compare the resolution price with the recorded entry price.
- Equal price means remain pending and retry.
- Standard win adds 1; standard loss subtracts 1.
- Persist scores and predictions in DynamoDB.
- Repeated requests must not create duplicate rounds or score twice.
- Browser closure must not stop backend resolution.
- Restore anonymous identity and active state after reopening.

## Agreed stack

- React, TypeScript and Vite.
- Tailwind CSS and Lucide.
- TanStack Query with HTTP polling.
- Zod at untrusted boundaries.
- API Gateway HTTP API.
- Focused TypeScript AWS Lambda handlers.
- DynamoDB.
- Step Functions Standard.
- Serverless Framework.
- Coinbase Exchange public BTC-USD API.
- AWS Amplify Hosting.
- Vitest, React Testing Library and Playwright.
- CloudWatch structured logs and actionable alarms.

Do not introduce CDK, WebSockets, user accounts or additional services
without explaining the tradeoff and agreeing the change with user.

## Engineering requirements

- Keep handlers thin and domain rules independently testable.
- Use decimal-safe price comparison.
- Validate requests and provider responses.
- Use conditional DynamoDB writes and transactions for concurrency.
- Make mutation and settlement effects idempotent.
- Keep display-price caching separate from settlement pricing.
- Use injected clocks/providers for deterministic tests.
- Never shorten the production minimum 60-second wait.
- Show honest loading, pending, stale-data and error states.
- Never commit secrets or log anonymous access tokens.
- Use least-privilege IAM where practical.
- Explain material changes to the agreed architecture before implementing.

## Optional features

Standard scoring remains +1/-1.
Double Down and Redemption are separate optional modes.
Implement them only after the MVP gate passes.
Document and test their scoring explicitly.
Use independent feature flags.

## Skills

At T01, install and verify Grill Me and Ponytail for Cursor.
Do not assume they are installed or automatically running.

- Grill Me: clarify important planning decisions before application code.
- Ponytail: review simplification after relevant tests pass.
- Preserve behaviour, contracts and correctness safeguards.
- Inspect proposed simplifications before applying them.

## Learning

user must be able to explain the implementation in an interview.
Explain important decisions, unfamiliar concepts and failure scenarios.
Do not hide complexity behind generated code or claim guarantees
the implementation does not provide.